Presidential elections were held in [[Senegal]] on 25 February 2007. Incumbent president [[Abdoulaye Wade]] was re-elected in the first round with almost 56% of the vote.<ref name="MG">Diadie Ba, [http://www.mg.co.za/articlePage.aspx?articleid=300733&area=/breaking_news/breaking_news__africa/ "Senegal's Wade re-elected, warns opposition"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071001010436/http://www.mg.co.za/articlePage.aspx?articleid=300733&area=%2Fbreaking_news%2Fbreaking_news__africa%2F |date=2007-10-01 }}, Reuters (''Mail & Guardian Online''), 2 March 2007.</ref><ref name=Final>[http://www.seneweb.com/news/elections2007/article.php?artid=9107 "Le texte intégral de la décision du Conseil constitutionnel"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070520234508/http://www.seneweb.com/news/elections2007/article.php?artid=9107 |date=20 May 2007 }}, Agence de Presse Sénégalaise (Seneweb.com), 11 March 2007 {{in lang|fr}}.</ref>

==Background==
Wade announced the date for the election on 13 April 2006.<ref>[http://www.liberal-international.org/newsletter.asp?ia_id=1284 "Senegal elections set for 25 February 2007"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160303203635/http://www.liberal-international.org/newsletter.asp?ia_id=1284 |date=3 March 2016 }}, Liberal International, Issue 35.</ref> The election campaign officially began on 4 February 2007.<ref name=Di/> Soldiers voted early on 17 February and 18 February; this was the first time in the country's history that soldiers were allowed to vote.<ref>[http://www.apanews.net/spip.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=21477 "Military and paramilitary voting ends in Senegal"]{{Dead link|date=April 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, African Press Agency, 18 February 2007.</ref>

Had a run-off been necessary, it would have been held on 18 March 2007.<ref>[http://news.monstersandcritics.com/africa/news/article_1267777.php/Preview_Senegalese_to_vote_for_president_in_Sunday_polls "Senegalese to vote for president in Sunday polls"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070929131323/http://news.monstersandcritics.com/africa/news/article_1267777.php/Preview_Senegalese_to_vote_for_president_in_Sunday_polls |date=2007-09-29 }}, DPA, 22 February 2007.</ref> A [[2007 Senegalese parliamentary election|parliamentary election]] was initially intended to be held on 25 February as well, but it was delayed to 3 June 2007.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.iol.co.za/news/africa/senegal-hopes-for-elections-in-june-311569 |url-status=live |access-date=10 June 2023 |title=Senegal hopes for elections in June |work=[[Independent Online]] |agency=Sapa-AFP (African Press Agency) |date=19 January 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230610094419/https://www.iol.co.za/news/africa/senegal-hopes-for-elections-in-june-311569 |archive-date=10 June 2023}}</ref>

==Candidates==
15 candidates ran for President, including Wade.<ref name=Di>[http://apanews.net/article.php3?id_article=20426 "La 1ère journée de campagne électorale peu animée au Sénégal"]{{dead link|date=May 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, African Press Agency, 4 February 2007 {{in lang|fr}}.</ref> Wade was nominated as the presidential candidate of his party, the [[Senegalese Democratic Party]] (PDS), on 15 October 2006.<ref>[http://www.apanews.net/spip.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=12903 "Wade nominated for February 2007 presidential elections"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071222063957/http://www.apanews.net/spip.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=12903 |date=2007-12-22 }}, African Press Agency, 16 October 2006.</ref> [[Idrissa Seck]], a former Prime Minister who was once considered Wade's protégé, also ran. Seck announced his candidacy for President in April 2006; he had been arrested in 2005 on charges of embezzlement and threatening state security but never faced a trial and was eventually released in February 2006.<ref>[http://www.irinnews.org/Report.aspx?ReportId=58670 "President’s onetime protégé to run for election"], IRIN, 5 April 2006.</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.iol.co.za/news/africa/seck-will-challenge-wade-in-senegal-election-272076 |url-status=live |access-date=10 June 2023 |title=Seck will challenge Wade in Senegal election|date=5 April 2006 |work=[[Independent Online]] |agency=[[Reuters]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230610095115/https://www.iol.co.za/news/africa/seck-will-challenge-wade-in-senegal-election-272076 |archive-date=10 June 2023}}</ref> On January 22, 2007, Wade said that Seck had agreed to return to the ruling PDS;<ref>{{Cite news |title=Wade makes peace with former prime minister |work=[[Independent Online]] |agency=[[Reuters]] |date=23 January 2007 |url=https://www.iol.co.za/news/africa/wade-makes-peace-with-former-prime-minister-311978 |url-status=live |access-date=10 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230610095453/https://www.iol.co.za/news/africa/wade-makes-peace-with-former-prime-minister-311978 |archive-date=10 June 2023}}</ref> Seck subsequently confirmed this, but said that he would remain a presidential candidate. If he had withdrawn his candidacy, the law would have required that the election be delayed,<ref>[http://www.apanews.net/spip.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=20237 "Former Senegalese Premier returns to ruling party, but insists on presidency"]{{dead link|date=May 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, African Press Agency, 1 February 2007.</ref> but shortly afterwards the National Assembly voted to change the law so that a delay would not be necessary in the event of a candidate's withdrawal.<ref>[http://www.apanews.net/spip.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=20776 "Senegalese MPs remove electoral article from constitution"]{{Dead link|date=April 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, African Press Agency, 8 February 2007.</ref>

[[Ousmane Tanor Dieng]] ran as the candidate of the former ruling [[Socialist Party of Senegal|Socialist Party]] (PS).<ref>[http://apanews.net/article.php3?id_article=20400 "Ousmane Tanor Dieng teste pour la première fois sa représentativité électorale"]{{dead link|date=May 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, African Press Agency, 4 February 2007 {{in lang|fr}}.</ref> Former Prime Minister [[Moustapha Niasse]] ran as the candidate of a coalition of opposition parties, including his own [[Alliance of the Forces of Progress (Senegal)|Alliance of the Forces of Progress]] (AFP).<ref>[http://www.apanews.net/spip.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=17442 "Senegalese opposition leader heads new coalition"]{{dead link|date=May 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, African Press Agency, 22 December 2006.</ref> Other candidates included [[Abdoulaye Bathily]] of the [[Democratic League/Movement for the Labour Party]] (LD/MPT),<ref name=Ancien>[http://www.apanews.net/spip.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=20437 "Abdoulaye Bathily, un ancien allié de Wade à l’assaut du pouvoir"]{{dead link|date=May 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, African Press Agency, 5 February 2007 {{in lang|fr}}.</ref> [[Landing Savané]], a Minister of State and the Secretary-General of [[And-Jëf/African Party for Democracy and Socialism]] (AJ/PADS),<ref name=Cand>[http://www.apanews.net/spip.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=14674 "Un allié de Me Wade candidat à la présidentielle de février 2007"]{{dead link|date=May 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, African Press Agency, 13 November 2006 {{in lang|fr}}.</ref> [[Ziguinchor]] Mayor [[Robert Sagna]],<ref>[http://apanews.net/spip.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=20520 "Robert Sagna running for presidency in Senegal"]{{Dead link|date=April 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, African Press Agency, 6 February 2007.</ref> and former ambassador [[Modou Dia]].<ref>[http://apanews.net/spip.php?page=show_article_eng&id_article=20414 "L’ancien ambassadeur Modou Dia veut faire décoller le Sénégal à l’horizon 2017"]{{Dead link|date=April 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, African Press Agency, 4 February 2007 {{in lang|fr}}.</ref>

==Campaign==
Wade faced criticism over a variety of issues, including unemployment and the continuing [[Casamance conflict]], as well as alleged corruption and the delays of the parliamentary election. Major construction projects undertaken by Wade split opinion in the country. Wade's campaign called for the people to give him a majority of the vote in the first round, but it was widely expected that a second round would be necessary, and Wade's opponents denied the possibility that he could legitimately win a first-round majority. Many politicians who had backed Wade in 2000 had since turned against him, which could be viewed as making it improbable that Wade would do as well in 2007; on the other hand, it has been pointed out that Senegal's demographics, with a high proportion of young people in the electorate, could make it difficult to compare the two elections.<ref>{{usurped|1=[https://web.archive.org/web/20070307092152/http://www.angus-reid.com/analysis/index.cfm/fuseaction/viewItem/itemID/14946 "Senegal surprises itself at the ballot box"]}}, Angus Reid Global Monitor, 5 March 2007.</ref>

==Results==
Shortly after the election was held, Prime Minister [[Macky Sall]], who was also Wade's campaign manager, said that Wade had won the election in the first round with about 57% of the vote, based on initial results.<ref>[http://apanews.net/article_eng.php3?id_article=22089 "Wade’s side claims victory in first round of Senegalese presidential poll"]{{dead link|date=May 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, African Press Agency, 26 February 2007.</ref> According to results announced on 1 March, Wade won about 1.9 million votes, about 55.9% of the total. Seck was in second place with 14.9% of the vote and Tanor Dieng was in third place with 13.6%. Voter turnout was said to be 70.5%.<ref name=MG/> Wade won the most votes in 32 out of 34 of the country's [[department (Senegal)|departments]], including a majority in 28 of them. Seck won the department of [[Thiès Department|Thiès]] and Niasse won the department of [[Nioro du Rip Department|Nioro]].<ref>[http://www.seneweb.com/news/elections2007/article.php?artid=8891 "Abdoulaye Wade passe au premier tour avec 55,07 %, selon des chiffres provisoires"], Agence de Presse Sénégalaise (Seneweb.com), 1 March 2007 {{in lang|fr}}.</ref>

Abdoulaye Bathily's campaign rejected the results and alleged that there were flaws in the voting, saying that a person could be registered more than once, and that the ink used in voting, which was supposed to be [[indelible ink|indelible]], could be washed off.<ref name=MG/> Ousmane Tanor Dieng's spokesperson also rejected the results,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.iol.co.za/news/africa/dieng-challenges-election-results-in-senegal-316927 |title=Dieng challenges election results in Senegal |agency=[[Reuters]] |work=[[Independent Online]] |date=28 February 2007 |access-date=10 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170914112730/https://www.iol.co.za/news/africa/dieng-challenges-election-results-in-senegal-316927 |archive-date=14 September 2017}}</ref> and on 5 March Dieng filed a Constitutional Court appeal regarding alleged lack of transparency and irregularities.<ref>[http://english.aljazeera.net/NR/exeres/7FD4AAB9-4D98-41AF-A986-0719F8214ADC.htm "Loser claims fraud in Senegal polls"], Al Jazeera, 5 March 2007.</ref>

Final results from the Constitutional Council on 11 March gave Wade 55.90%, Seck 14.92%, and Dieng 13.56%. The Council rejected appeals from Dieng and Bathily.<ref name=Final/> Wade was sworn in on 3 April at the [[Leopold Sedar Senghor Stadium]] in Dakar, with many African leaders and about 60,000 spectators in attendance.<ref>{{cite news |title=Wade sworn in for second term |url=https://www.iol.co.za/news/africa/wade-sworn-in-for-second-term-321635 |access-date=10 June 2023 |agency=Sapa-AFP |work=[[Independent Online]] |date=4 April 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230610092050/https://www.iol.co.za/news/africa/wade-sworn-in-for-second-term-321635 |archive-date=10 June 2023}}</ref>

